<% content_for :title, "Internationalization" %>
<div class="mb-8">
  <p class="mb-2 text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider text-primary">Getting Started</p>
  <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight mb-3">Internationalization</h1>
  <p class="text-muted-foreground">
    Two kinds of words, translated in two places. This page is about the second kind.
  </p>
</div>

<div class="rounded-lg border border-border p-5 mb-8">
  <p class="text-sm mb-3">
    <strong>Content</strong> is what your call site writes: a field's label, a button's text, a select's
    options, a modal's title, a toast's message. It's yours, and you translate it the way you translate
    everything else — <code class="text-xs bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded">t(".x")</code>,
    <code class="text-xs bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded">human_attribute_name</code>, your own locale files.
    <strong>The kit never ships a translation for content.</strong> Model-aware Field and Select's enum labels
    already work this way: they resolve through Rails' own label and attribute lookups, so your locale files
    translate them and the kit adds nothing.
  </p>
  <p class="text-sm mb-0">
    <strong>Chrome</strong> is what the kit says on its own, that no call site ever writes: a toast's close-button
    accessible name, Select's "No results" and its result count, the unsaved-changes prompt. You don't pass these
    because you don't know they exist. <strong>Only chrome lives in
    <code class="text-xs bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded">rails_ui_kit.en.yml</code></strong> — the handful of strings listed under Keys below,
    several of them accessible names, which is the only thing a screen reader announces. It is not a translation
    of the components.
  </p>
</div>

<h2 class="text-base font-semibold mb-3">Where the strings live</h2>
<p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground mb-3">
  <code class="text-xs bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded">config/locales/rails_ui_kit.en.yml</code>, shipped in the gem. Rails engines add their own
  <code class="text-xs bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded">config/locales</code> to <code class="text-xs bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded">I18n.load_path</code> automatically — there's no initializer to add and nothing to
  require. To override a string, add the same key under <code class="text-xs bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded">rails_ui_kit.*</code> to any YAML file in
  your own app's <code class="text-xs bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded">config/locales</code>. Your application's load path is added after every engine's, so your
  value wins — no monkey-patching, no forking the gem:
</p>
<div class="rounded-lg overflow-hidden border border-border mb-8">
  <div class="flex items-center justify-between bg-muted px-4 py-2">
    <span class="text-xs text-muted-foreground">config/locales/rails_ui_kit.en.yml — in your app, not the gem</span>
    <button data-copy-target="#i18n-override" class="text-xs text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground transition-colors">Copy</button>
  </div>
  <pre class="bg-card px-5 py-4 overflow-x-auto text-sm leading-relaxed" tabindex="0"><code id="i18n-override" class="text-card-foreground"><%= h(example_i18n_override) %></code></pre>
</div>

<h2 class="text-base font-semibold mb-3">Keys</h2>
<p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground mb-3">
  Every chrome string the kit renders. Each one resolves <strong>call site → your locale file → the kit's default</strong>: the
  keyword is the key's last segment, so if you know one you know the other.
</p>
<div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-lg border border-border mb-8">
  <table class="w-full text-sm">
    <thead class="bg-muted">
      <tr>
        <th class="text-start px-4 py-3 text-xs font-semibold text-muted-foreground uppercase tracking-wider">Key</th>
        <th class="text-start px-4 py-3 text-xs font-semibold text-muted-foreground uppercase tracking-wider">Default (English)</th>
        <th class="text-start px-4 py-3 text-xs font-semibold text-muted-foreground uppercase tracking-wider">Per-instance keyword</th>
        <th class="text-start px-4 py-3 text-xs font-semibold text-muted-foreground uppercase tracking-wider">Rendered by</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody class="divide-y divide-border">
      <% [
        ["rails_ui_kit.modal.unsaved_changes_title",    "Unsaved Changes",         "unsaved_changes_title:",   "Ui::ModalComponent(track_changes: true) — the confirm dialog it opens on a dirty close"],
        ["rails_ui_kit.modal.unsaved_changes_message",  "You have unsaved changes. Are you sure you want to close?", "unsaved_changes_message:", "same"],
        ["rails_ui_kit.confirm_dialog.title",           "Confirmation required",   "title:",                   "Ui::ConfirmDialogComponent"],
        ["rails_ui_kit.confirm_dialog.message",         "Are you sure?",           "message:",                 "Ui::ConfirmDialogComponent"],
        ["rails_ui_kit.confirm_dialog.confirm_label",   "Confirm",                 "confirm_label:",           "Ui::ConfirmDialogComponent"],
        ["rails_ui_kit.confirm_dialog.cancel_label",    "Cancel",                  "cancel_label:",            "Ui::ConfirmDialogComponent"],
        ["rails_ui_kit.toast.close_label",              "Close notification",      "close_label:",             "Ui::ToastComponent — the dismiss button's accessible name. Pass it to Ui::ToastContainerComponent as well, so toasts JavaScript creates get it too"],
        ["rails_ui_kit.toast.default_title",            "Notification",            "default_title:",           "Ui::ToastComponent(message: nil) and window.triggerToast(type) with no message"],
        ["rails_ui_kit.toast.actions_hint",             "Press F8 to reach its actions.", "actions_hint:",     "Ui::ToastComponent and Ui::ToastContainerComponent — announced after a toast that has actions"],
        ["rails_ui_kit.toast.region_label",             "Notifications",           "region_label:",            "Ui::ToastContainerComponent — the name of the toast stack's landmark, which F8 reaches"],
        ["rails_ui_kit.select.search_placeholder",      "Search…",                 "search_placeholder:",      "Ui::SelectComponent(search: true) — the placeholder in the field at the top of the popup"],
        ["rails_ui_kit.select.no_results",              "No results",              "no_results:",              "Ui::SelectComponent(search: true) — shown when the filter matches nothing"],
        ["rails_ui_kit.select.clear_label",             "Clear",                   "clear_label:",             "Ui::SelectComponent(prompt:) — the clear button's own word, in front of the field's name"],
        ["rails_ui_kit.select.results",               "%{count} result / %{count} results", "results:",       "Ui::SelectComponent(search: true) — announced once typing settles. Give every plural category your locale uses; the browser picks with Intl.PluralRules"],
        ["rails_ui_kit.choices.required_message",       "Select at least one option.", "required_message:",   "Ui::ChoicesComponent(multiple: true, required: true) — the screen-reader hint beside the * and the message ui--choices gives the browser"],
        ["rails_ui_kit.field.required_label",           "required",                "required_label:",         "Ui::FieldComponent — a hidden span beside the label's *, read into the accessible name of search-mode Select's trigger only, the one control ARIA bars aria-required on"],
        ["rails_ui_kit.character_counter.count",        "%{count} / %{limit}",     "count:",                   "Ui::FieldComponent — the visible count at the end of the description line, on a Textarea(counter: true, limit:)"],
        ["rails_ui_kit.character_counter.remaining",    "%{count} character remaining / %{count} characters remaining", "remaining:", "Ui::FieldComponent — announced once when the room left first reaches ten percent of the limit"],
        ["rails_ui_kit.character_counter.over",         "%{count} character over the limit / %{count} characters over the limit", "over:", "Ui::FieldComponent — announced once when the count first exceeds the limit, and once when it comes back under"],
        ["rails_ui_kit.turbo_disable_with.processing",  "Processing...",           "data-turbo-disable-with",  "no owning component — render it into the meta tag yourself, see below"],
        ["rails_ui_kit.dark_mode.switch_to_light",      "Switch to light mode",    "data-ui--dark-mode-light-label-value", "no owning component — render it into the toggle button yourself, see below"],
        ["rails_ui_kit.dark_mode.switch_to_dark",       "Switch to dark mode",     "data-ui--dark-mode-dark-label-value",  "same"],
      ].each do |key, default, keyword, used_by| %>
        <tr class="hover:bg-muted/50">
          <td class="px-4 py-3 font-mono text-xs text-primary whitespace-nowrap"><%= key %></td>
          <td class="px-4 py-3 text-muted-foreground whitespace-nowrap"><%= default %></td>
          <td class="px-4 py-3 font-mono text-xs text-muted-foreground whitespace-nowrap"><%= keyword %></td>
          <td class="px-4 py-3 text-muted-foreground"><%= used_by %></td>
        </tr>
      <% end %>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>

<h2 class="text-base font-semibold mb-3">Ruby components</h2>
<p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground mb-3">
  A component that owns a string resolves it through <code class="text-xs bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded">I18n.t</code> at render time — so a locale
  switch between renders takes effect — and accepts a keyword that overrides it, same as every
  other attribute in the kit:
</p>
<div class="rounded-lg overflow-hidden border border-border mb-8">
  <div class="flex items-center justify-between bg-muted px-4 py-2">
    <span class="text-xs text-muted-foreground">ERB</span>
    <button data-copy-target="#i18n-ruby" class="text-xs text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground transition-colors">Copy</button>
  </div>
  <pre class="bg-card px-5 py-4 overflow-x-auto text-sm leading-relaxed" tabindex="0"><code id="i18n-ruby" class="text-card-foreground"><%= h(example_i18n_ruby_override) %></code></pre>
</div>

<h2 class="text-base font-semibold mb-3">Stimulus controllers</h2>
<p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground mb-3">
  A Stimulus controller can't call <code class="text-xs bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded">I18n.t</code>. Where a controller's string is owned by one of the kit's
  own components (Modal, ConfirmDialog, Toast), that component renders the translated value as a
  Stimulus value or data attribute, and the controller reads it — falling back to the English
  literal only when the attribute is missing, so hand-written markup that predates this still
  works. <code class="text-xs bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded">Ui::ConfirmDialogComponent</code> is the clearest example: it renders the same title and
  message both as visible text and as <code class="text-xs bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded">data-default-title</code>/<code class="text-xs bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded">data-default-message</code>, and
  <code class="text-xs bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded">ui--dialog</code> reads those back rather than keeping its own English-only copy — call
  <code class="text-xs bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded">window.defaultConfirmDialog()</code> with no argument and it shows whatever title and message Ruby
  rendered, in whatever locale rendered them.
</p>
<p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground mb-3">
  <code class="text-xs bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded">ui--turbo-disable-with</code> and <code class="text-xs bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded">ui--dark-mode</code> are different: neither is rendered by a kit component —
  a host wires each one up on its own markup — so there's no Ruby render call to source the string
  from automatically. Render the translation into the attribute yourself:
</p>
<div class="rounded-lg overflow-hidden border border-border mb-8">
  <div class="flex items-center justify-between bg-muted px-4 py-2">
    <span class="text-xs text-muted-foreground">ERB</span>
    <button data-copy-target="#i18n-standalone" class="text-xs text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground transition-colors">Copy</button>
  </div>
  <pre class="bg-card px-5 py-4 overflow-x-auto text-sm leading-relaxed" tabindex="0"><code id="i18n-standalone" class="text-card-foreground"><%= h(example_i18n_standalone_controllers) %></code></pre>
</div>

<h2 class="text-base font-semibold mb-3">Plural categories</h2>
<p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground mb-3">
  A count's wording is the language's business, not the kit's. English has two plural categories,
  French two with different boundaries (0 takes <code class="text-xs bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded">one</code>), Russian four and
  Arabic six. So Ruby hands the browser <em>every</em> form the locale defines, plus the locale that
  rendered them, and the browser picks with
  <code class="text-xs bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded">Intl.PluralRules</code> once the count is known. Write every form with
  <code class="text-xs bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded">%{count}</code> in it — <code class="text-xs bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded">one</code>
  is a category, not the number one:
</p>
<div class="rounded-lg overflow-hidden border border-border mb-8">
  <div class="flex items-center justify-between bg-muted px-4 py-2">
    <span class="text-xs text-muted-foreground">config/locales/rails_ui_kit.ru.yml — in your app</span>
    <button data-copy-target="#i18n-plurals" class="text-xs text-muted-foreground hover:text-foreground transition-colors">Copy</button>
  </div>
  <pre class="bg-card px-5 py-4 overflow-x-auto text-sm leading-relaxed" tabindex="0"><code id="i18n-plurals" class="text-card-foreground"><%= h(example_i18n_plurals) %></code></pre>
</div>

<p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground mb-3">
  Type one of <code class="text-xs bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded">q1-</code>,
  <code class="text-xs bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded">q2-</code>,
  <code class="text-xs bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded">q3-</code>,
  <code class="text-xs bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded">q11-</code> or
  <code class="text-xs bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded">q100-</code> below to narrow the list to that many
  options. The count is announced in the field's status region, which is what a screen reader reads:
</p>
<div id="plural-preview" class="rounded-lg border border-border bg-background p-8 mb-8">
  <%= render(Ui::FieldComponent.new(name: "plural_demo")) do |field| %>
    <% field.with_label { "Plural demo" } %>
    <% field.with_control(Ui::SelectComponent, options: DocsController.plural_demo_options, search: true, include_blank: true) %>
  <% end %>
  <p id="plural-preview-status" class="mt-3 text-sm text-muted-foreground" aria-hidden="true"></p>
</div>

<h2 class="text-base font-semibold mb-3">When words get longer</h2>
<p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground mb-3">
  German runs about 30% longer than English, and a pseudo-locale pass runs 40% longer. Controls
  hold their height — a row of controls lining up is what the height scale is for — so length has to
  go somewhere else. What the kit guarantees: a button grows sideways and never wraps; a closed
  select truncates the way a native select does, and the full text is still in its accessible name and
  in the open list; <strong>the open list wraps</strong>, because a truncated option in a popup that
  matches the control's width is text you have no way to read; and a tooltip wraps at a maximum width
  rather than running off the screen.
</p>
<div id="expansion-preview" class="rounded-lg border border-border bg-background p-8 mb-8 space-y-4">
  <%# A field's real width, not the page's: an option only has to wrap when the control is the %>
  <%# size a form actually gives it. %>
  <div class="max-w-sm">
    <%= render(Ui::FieldComponent.new(name: "expansion_demo")) do |field| %>
      <% field.with_label { "A select whose options outgrow it" } %>
      <% field.with_control(Ui::SelectComponent, options: DocsController.expansion_demo_options, include_blank: true) %>
    <% end %>
  </div>
  <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-4">
    <%= render(Ui::ButtonComponent.new) { "Änderungen übernehmen und fortfahren" } %>
    <%= render(Ui::TooltipComponent.new(text: "Die Zahlungsbedingungen gelten ab dem Tag, an dem die Bestellung bestätigt wurde.")) do |tooltip| %>
      <% tooltip.with_trigger do %>
        <%= render(Ui::ButtonComponent.new(variant: :outline)) { "Hover for a long tooltip" } %>
      <% end %>
    <% end %>
  </div>
</div>

<h2 class="text-base font-semibold mb-3">Switching locale</h2>
<p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground mb-3">
  This example app doesn't wire up locale switching itself — it ships one locale, English — but
  every string above resolves through <code class="text-xs bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded">I18n.locale</code> the same as any other Rails string. Set it the
  way you already do (a <code class="text-xs bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded">before_action</code>, a route scope, <code class="text-xs bg-muted px-1 py-0.5 rounded">ActiveSupport::CurrentAttributes</code>) and the kit's
  own components follow it.
</p>
